<p class="eyebrow">Privacy · V2 v1.2.2</p>
<h1>A minimal product questionnaire, including some younger participants</h1>
<p class="lead">The questionnaire is used to make concrete app-design decisions. It is neither a diagnostic tool nor medical research.</p>
<h2>What is stored</h2>
<div class="grid"><div class="module"><strong>Answers</strong><span>Closed choices about interface and features.</span></div><div class="module"><strong>Pseudonymous session</strong><span>A private token enables save, resume and deletion. No account.</span></div><div class="module"><strong>Cohort</strong><span>The database distinguishes adult / minor only. Exact country is not stored as an answer.</span></div><div class="module"><strong>Not requested</strong><span>Name, date of birth, diagnosis, medication, school, workplace or participant email.</span></div></div>
<h2>Ages 13–17: regional routing</h2>
<p>Country is asked before a session is created only to choose the privacy route. The exact selection stays in the browser. The server validates only a <strong>coarse eligibility code</strong> and does not persist it in questionnaire tables.</p>
<ul><li><strong>France, Belgium, Luxembourg:</strong> ages 13–17 accepted without a parent email.</li><li><strong>United States:</strong> ages 13–17 accepted; under 13 excluded.</li><li><strong>Canada outside Quebec:</strong> ages 13–17 accepted.</li><li><strong>Quebec:</strong> ages 14–17 accepted; at age 13 the survey stops without asking for parent contact details.</li><li><strong>Switzerland:</strong> ages 14–17 accepted for this round; age 13 is not currently accepted. This is a cautious project rule, not a universal Swiss legal threshold.</li><li><strong>Other countries:</strong> minors are not currently accepted.</li></ul>
<h2>Legal basis — France, Belgium, Luxembourg</h2>
<p>For younger participants in these countries, minimal processing relies on <strong>legitimate interests</strong> under GDPR Article 6(1)(f): testing concrete design choices before development while avoiding identifying or health information.</p>
<p>The route does not rely on Article 6(1)(a) consent as the GDPR legal basis. Article 8 provides special rules where consent is the basis used for an online service offered to a child. Younger participants still merit specific protection and an enhanced balancing assessment.</p>
<div class="soft"><strong>Voluntary participation is not the same as “consent” as the GDPR legal basis.</strong><br>The participation checkbox records the young person’s choice and understanding; it is not presented as the GDPR legal basis for the supported-EU minor route.</div>
<h2>Safeguards</h2><ul><li>no diagnosis or medication questions;</li><li>no free text;</li><li>no account, advertising, commercial profiling or third-party analytics;</li><li>no young participant or parent email in the new route;</li><li>optional modules clearly separated;</li><li>exact country not retained;</li><li>response deletion while the private token remains available.</li></ul>
<h2>Objection and deletion</h2>
<p>Where legitimate interests are used, the person can object to processing. Here the practical mechanism is the <strong>“Delete my response”</strong> button available from the pause screen and after submission while this browser still holds the private session token.</p>
<p>If the token is lost, deliberate lack of identifiers may make it technically impossible to identify the relevant response without collecting more personal data.</p>
<h2>Legacy guardian route</h2><p>From v1.2.2 onward, <strong>no new guardian request</strong> is created. Existing private links already sent remain functional so historical withdrawal options are preserved.</p>
<h2>Retention</h2><ul><li>unfinished sessions: eligible for deletion after 30 days of inactivity;</li><li>submitted answers: up to 12 months for this cycle, then deletion or irreversible aggregation;</li><li>legacy pending guardian requests: expiry after 7 days under the existing mechanism.</li></ul>
<p class="small">A cautious approach for the current minimal scope, not universal legal certification. New countries, health data, free text, accounts, profiling or advertising require a fresh review.</p>
